\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A few framing notes before the interpretation, since they change how much weight to put on any single number:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 These trait labels come from personality and organizational psychology research (sometimes grouped as the "Dark Tetrad" \'97 Narcissism, Machiavellianism, Psychopathy, Sadism \'97 alongside Empathy, Humility, and Ethical Leadership). They describe tendencies everyone sits somewhere on, not clinical diagnoses. A workplace "Psychopathy" score is a different thing entirely from a clinical diagnosis, which would require a licensed clinician.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This is self-report data \'97 it reflects how you see yourself, which can run ahead of how colleagues would rate you, especially on traits like Humility. I'll flag where that gap matters most.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 None of your seven scores are particularly low (they range 43\'9675). That means the most useful signal is usually the 
\f1\i relative ranking
\f0\i0  within your own profile, not the absolute numbers \'97 and it's worth knowing that an all-elevated pattern like this is also consistent with a generally agreeable response style on the questionnaire itself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 1. What this profile suggests\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The single most interesting thing in your numbers: 
\f2\b Psychopathy (68) and Ethical Leadership (68) are identical.
\f0\b0  That tension is really the headline of your whole profile \'97 strong capacity for calculated, unsentimental, sometimes risk-tolerant action, paired with an equally strong self-reported commitment to doing it the right way. Nothing in the scores tells you which one wins when they conflict. That's the open question, and it's what determines whether this profile plays out as "effective and principled" or "effective at other people's expense" under real pressure.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Leadership style.
\f0\b0  You likely read as a bold, strategic operator: comfortable with conflict and high-stakes calls, good at reading organizational politics (Machiavellianism 64), and calm rather than anxious under pressure (a common feature of elevated Psychopathy, particularly its "boldness" component). That's held in check by genuine counterweights \'97 high Humility (75) suggests you don't need to be the center of the story, moderate Empathy (61) means you're not indifferent to people's experience, and low Narcissism (46) and low Sadism (43) suggest the sharper traits are goal-driven, not ego- or cruelty-driven.\

\f2\b Biases to watch for.
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Self-serving interpretation of your own tactics
\f0\i0  \'97 because you score high on Humility and Ethical Leadership, you may extend yourself the benefit of the doubt on a strategic move faster than you'd extend it to a colleague doing the same thing.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Projected calculation
\f0\i0  \'97 elevated Machiavellianism is linked to reading more strategic intent into others' behavior than is actually there ("they must be angling for something").\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Confidence-as-evidence
\f0\i0  \'97 staying calm under pressure can feel like being right. It isn't; it's worth treating your own certainty as a prompt to double-check rather than a stopping point.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Workplace triggers.
\f0\b0  Competitive or political environments; tight deadlines where the clean path is slower than the clever one; pushback from someone with less power than you; moments that call for sitting with someone's emotion rather than solving it; public credit or recognition moments.\

\f2\b Risk points, in short.
\f0\b0  The core pattern isn't "doesn't care" \'97 your Ethical Leadership and Humility scores argue against that. It's a gap between strategic capability and whether the checks on that capability get consulted 
\f1\i in the moment
\f0\i0 , not just afterward.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Top 3 risks to monitor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 1. Ends-justify-means drift under pressure.
\f0\b0  Elevated Machiavellianism and Psychopathy together are the combination most linked to comfort with instrumental reasoning \'97 selective information-sharing, timing, or framing that serves the goal more than it serves transparency. Watch for decisions you'd explain differently to your boss than to the person directly affected by them.\

\f2\b 2. Empathy that stays cognitive instead of becoming behavioral.
\f0\b0  Your Empathy score is solid but sits next to a meaningfully higher Psychopathy score. Research on psychopathy usually separates 
\f1\i understanding
\f0\i0  what someone feels from being 
\f1\i moved
\f0\i0  by it enough to change course. You may accurately predict that a decision will land badly and go ahead unchanged anyway.\

\f2\b 3. Boldness that outruns other people's risk tolerance.
\f0\b0  Low baseline anxiety under pressure is genuinely useful \'97 but it also means you may not feel the warning signal a more cautious colleague feels, and can mistake their caution for slowness rather than for real signal.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 3. Top 3 strengths to build on\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 1. Humility (75) \'97 your highest score, and the best check on Risk 1.
\f0\b0  Humility predicts willingness to admit error and actually update after feedback rather than defend a position. If you build habitual feedback-seeking into your week, your humility gives that feedback somewhere real to land.\

\f2\b 2. Empathy (61) + Ethical Leadership (68) together \'97 your material for closing the gap in Risk 2.
\f0\b0  You're not empathy-blind, and you hold a real standard for how outcomes should be reached. The fix isn't building empathy from zero; it's building a habit that gives the empathy you already have a chance to actually change your next move.\

\f2\b 3. Low Narcissism (46) and low Sadism (43) \'97 the most protective pairing here, and your check on Risk 3.
\f0\b0  A leader whose boldness serves ego digs in when challenged, because backing down threatens self-image. A leader whose boldness serves a goal \'97 which your low Narcissism suggests is closer to you \'97 can update when shown a better path to the same goal, because identity isn't on the line. That receptivity is worth actively using, not just having.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 4. A 4-week improvement plan\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Week 1 \'97 Notice the fork before you take it
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  Before any consequential ask or decision, pause 30 seconds and write one line: "What's the most direct way to get this outcome, and am I choosing something else?" You don't have to change course \'97 just notice when you're taking the strategic route over the direct one.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question:
\f0\i0  Was there a moment this week I chose the persuasive path over the direct one? What made that feel necessary at the time?\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workplace behavior:
\f0\i0  Ask one person \'97 ideally someone with less incentive to flatter you \'97 the feedback script below, about one specific decision from this week.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Week 2 \'97 Turn understanding into pacing
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  In your next two difficult conversations, reflect the other person's feeling back before offering your own view, and wait for a real answer before pivoting.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question:
\f0\i0  When someone pushed back on me this week, did I respond to their emotion, or only to their argument?\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workplace behavior:
\f0\i0  Delay one decision you were ready to make quickly by 24 hours, specifically to collect one dissenting view first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Week 3 \'97 Pressure-test your own certainty
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  For one decision where you feel calm and confident while others hesitate, ask the most cautious person in the room what failure mode they're picturing \'97 and let their answer change one detail of the plan.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question:
\f0\i0  Where did I feel impatient with caution this week \'97 and was that impatience earned, or was I discounting a real signal I didn't personally feel?\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workplace behavior:
\f0\i0  Publicly attribute credit for a result to whoever did the work, before anyone asks you to.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Week 4 \'97 Repair and build the loop
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment:
\f0\i0  Identify one moment where your pace or tactics may have landed harder than intended, and initiate a short repair conversation without waiting to be asked.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection question:
\f0\i0  Which pattern from the last four weeks showed up more than once \'97 and what standing structure would catch it automatically, rather than relying on me remembering to self-check?\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Workplace behavior:
\f0\i0  Put that structure in place \'97 e.g., ask one specific colleague to be your ongoing "tell me if this looks off" person, not just for this month.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 5. Scripts for key moments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Seeking feedback:
\f0\b0  "I'm working on how I [specific behavior]. Can you think of a recent moment it didn't land well, and tell me straight? I'd rather hear it now than later."\

\f2\b Pausing before a reactive decision:
\f0\b0  Internally \'97 "This can wait ten minutes. What am I not seeing yet?" Out loud \'97 "That's worth getting right. Let me sit with it and come back to you by [specific time]."\

\f2\b Giving credit:
\f0\b0  "Before we move on \'97 this worked because [name] did [specific thing]. I want that on the record, not just the result."\

\f2\b Repairing harm:
\f0\b0  "I've been thinking about [specific moment]. I think I moved faster than I should have and didn't fully weigh how it landed for you. I'm sorry \'97 can you tell me the impact, and what would help going forward?"\

\f2\b Responding to conflict:
\f0\b0  "I see this differently than you do, and I want to actually understand your reasoning before I respond. Can you walk me through how you got there?"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 6. Questions to ask yourself before applying any of this\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Does my culture actually reward directness and pausing, or quietly punish it? Match the experiment to the real environment, not the ideal one.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Who around me will tell me the truth if I ask \'97 and if I can't name someone, is building that relationship the real first step?\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Is this a moment where speed genuinely matters, or is my urgency a feeling rather than a fact?\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Am I doing this because I believe it, or to be seen doing it? Only one of those holds up under pressure.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What's the power gap between me and the person involved \'97 does that change how a pause or a direct question will land on them?\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Is there a real safety, legal, or compliance stake here that needs more than a personal growth experiment \'97 i.e., a second person's sign-off, not just my own reflection?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 7. Closing summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Nothing here points to a leader who's a risk because they don't care \'97 your Ethical Leadership and Humility scores argue against that reading. It points to a leader with real strategic and stress-tolerant capability whose main job is making sure the checks on that capability run in real time, under pressure, not just in hindsight. That's a practice-based gap, not a character flaw, and the plan above is built to close it one checkable habit at a time.\
}